ing. That knight attacks d8, f8, c5, g5, etc. So Black\'s king on f8 is already under potential attack from that knight. That makes king moves dangerous.) Actually NWF1 at e6 attacks f8, so king on f8 is in check? Wait, check means king is under attack — here knight on e6 attacks f8 (king\'s square). So Black king is in check! But the given valid moves do not include any move that gets out of check except possibly moving the king. Let\'s verify: Knight from e6 attacks f8 (one square away in L-shape: e6 to f8 is 2 up, 1 right). Yes, king on f8 is attacked by White knight on e6. Therefore Black is in check and must move out of check. That means the only legal moves are those that remove the check: moving the king, blocking, or capturing the knight.  \n\nValid moves given:  \n- B: c8-e6 (blocks? Bishop to e6 would be between knight and king? Knight on e6, bishop to e6 would capture the knight — yes! Bishop moves from c8 to e6 captures knight on e6. That removes the check and develops a piece. Excellent.)  \n- K: f8-e7 (moves king out of check to e7, but e7 is attacked by knight? Knight from e6 attacks e7? Actually knight from e6 attacks d8, f8, c5, g5, g7, f4, d4, c7 — not e7. So e7 is safe from that knight. But e7 may be exposed to other pieces later.)  \n- K: f8-e8 (king moves to e8, still attacked by knight? Knight from e6 attacks e8? e6 to e8 is 2 squares up, not L shape, so not attacked. So e8 is safe from knight. But then king is in the center and blocks rook.)  \n- K: f8-f7 (king moves to f7, attacked by knight? Knight from e6 attacks f7? e6 to f7 is 1 down, 1 right — that’s not L shape; L shape is 2+1. So not attacked. So f7 is safe from knight. But f7 might be attacked by bishop on c4? Yes, bishop on c4 attacks f7. So moving king to f7 would put it under bishop attack — but it\'s not check if bishop is not currently attacking f7? Wait, bishop on c4 attacks f7 along diagonal c4–f7. But is there any piece blocking? The board shows pawn on e6? Actually e6 is occupied by knight (White), so diagonal c4–f7: c4, d5, e6, f7. e6 has White knight, which blocks bishop\'s line to f7. So bishop cannot attack f7 currently because knight is in the way. So f7 is safe for king.)  \n- K: f8-g8 (king moves to g8, knight from e6 attacks g8? e6 to g8 is 2 up, 2 right — not L shape. So safe. But then king is still blocking rook.)  \n\nGiven the check, best is to capture the knight with bishop: B: c8-e6. This removes the attacker, develops a piece, opens the possibility of castling later, and avoids moving the king. Moving the king would delay castling and possibly expose it.  \n\n**Reason**: Capturing the knight eliminates an active White piece and gets out of check while developing. Moving the king would leave it in the center and lose castling rights or expose it unnecessarily.  \n\nAnswer: B: c8-e6'}
